Notebook[{
Cell["ContinuumLimit_EqualAccuracy", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"de2a74f0-edc4-47b8-98e6-898a34761c17"],

Cell["ContinuumLimit_EqualAccuracy.wl", "Subtitle",ExpressionUUID->"2703afb8-88b0-4cd2-bfc1-90877fcbf556"],

Cell["\<\
The Galerkin voxel against the gradient hierarchy at equal accuracy, on the \
exact layer.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b7b5dd96-b297-46f7-b926-cf5662e7be09"],

Cell["\<\
QUESTION.  The Galerkin voxel of degree p (Legendre moments of the field and \
its strain, tested against the same polynomials) converges on the layer at \
order 2p + 2.  The gradient hierarchy (the field a Taylor polynomial about \
each cell centre to the q-th gradient, the equation and its first q \
derivatives imposed at the centre: point matching) converges, with both \
contrasts, at order 2 for q = 1 and 2, 4 for q = 3 and 4, 6 for q = 5 and 6, \
8 for q = 7.  At the same order, how many cells, and how many unknowns, does \
each need for a given error?\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e7b28edf-a696-4d41-82ec-f11fb975487e"],

Cell["\<\
MODEL.  The layer of ContinuumLimit_SecondMoment.wl and \
ContinuumLimit_GradientHierarchy.wl, which are one model (checked below): 2 m \
thick, alpha = 5000 m/s, rho = 2500 kg/m^3, contrasts Delta rho = 100 kg/m^3 \
and Delta M = Delta lambda + 2 Delta mu = 4e9 Pa, omega = 300 rad/s, P at \
normal incidence, reflection and transmission read at z = -1 and 4.  Each \
scheme is run by its own script's solver, loaded into a context of its own so \
that their names cannot collide: gal`solveLayerP[om, n, p + 1] (2 (p + 1) \
unknowns a cell, 40 digits) and hier`solveHier[om, n, q, drho, dm] (q + 1 \
unknowns a cell, 60 digits).  The hierarchy's closed-form cell integrals are \
defined there for monomials to degree 6; the degree-7 one that q = 7 needs is \
added here by the same formula.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a101c9ff-8448-4873-bb4f-c764502fa3be"],

Cell["\<\
MEASURE.  The error of a run is the larger of |discrete/exact - 1| in \
reflection and transmission. The cells needed for a target error are read off \
each ladder by linear interpolation of log n against log(error) between the \
two grids that bracket it (exact for a power law); a target outside a ladder, \
or one the ladder crosses more than once, is refused, never extrapolated (the \
rules of scripts/equal_accuracy.py).  Unknowns = cells x unknowns a cell.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"40e9ac08-6e01-485e-8af7-9d1a4f076390"],

Cell["Writes ContinuumLimit_equal_accuracy.json.", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"55465ff7-633b-4007-a620-b186917cfd1f"],

Cell["\<\
both scripts are loaded first, each in its own context and silently: they \
define clashing globals\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f256eee5-4afd-4a82-a7b3-68ab5aec39bf"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

